.TH NEOXD 1 "November 2026" "neoxd 1.1.0" "User Manual"
.SH NAME
neoxd - hex dumper and analyzer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B neoxd
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIFILE\fR|\fB-\fR] [\fIFILE2\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B neoxd
is a modern replacement for standard UNIX hex dumpers like \fBxxd\fR and \fBhexdump\fR.
It is a performant, feature-full hex dumper and binary analysis utility written in Nim, engineered as a direct replacement for traditional UNIX tools. While traditional dumpers present raw binary data as a static grid of hex bytes, \fBneoxd\fR treats binary data as a structured, dynamic information stream. It pairs raw inspection with real-time statistical entropy analysis (using Shannon entropy with horizontal fractional sub-block visualizers), automatic magic signature and file type detection, structural summarization, side-by-side binary diffing, dump reversal/restoration, and proactive CLI guardrails (PRUFER).
.PP
In version 1.1.0, signature detection was expanded with structural parsing support for PE and Mach-O executable binaries, ensuring read-only section headers are correctly bypassed to eliminate false-positive signature tags. Interactive output natively pipes to \fB$PAGER\fR (defaulting to \fBless \-RFX\fR) with robust SIGPIPE signal handling.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-cols\fR, \fB\-\-columns\fR=\fIINT\fR
Number of octets per line (default: 16).
.TP
\fB\-w\fR, \fB\-\-window\fR=\fIINT\fR
Set the sliding window size for entropy calculation (default: 256).
.TP
\fB\-g\fR, \fB\-\-group\fR=\fIINT\fR
Number of bytes per hex group in output (default: 8; valid divisors include 1, 2, 4, 8, 16).
.TP
\fB\-S\fR, \fB\-\-search\fR=\fIPATTERN\fR
Search for and highlight occurrences of a hexadecimal byte sequence (e.g., \fB414243\fR) or an ASCII text string across chunk boundaries.
.TP
\fB\-\-anomalies\fR
Enable structural anomaly detection mode, filtering output to display only blocks where entropy shifts drastically or crosses a delta threshold.
.TP
\fB\-\-anomaly\-delta\fR=\fIFLOAT\fR
Minimum rolling entropy change delta required to trigger an anomaly block output (default: 1.5, range: 0.0 to 8.0).
.TP
\fB\-\-diff\fR
Compare two binary files side-by-side in hex diff mode. Highlight changed or differing bytes in color. Automatically triggered if two positional target file arguments are provided.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-summary\fR
Scan the target file and output a comprehensive file summary report, including entropy classification, structural block density, byte frequency histogram, and embedded magic headers (with false-positive filtering for executable segments).
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR=\fIFILE\fR
Write output directly to \fIFILE\fR. Disables ANSI color codes automatically unless \fB\-\-color\fR is explicitly set.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR
Disable ANSI color escape sequences.
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR
Force ANSI color escape sequences even when piping output or writing to a file.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-pager\fR
Disable automatic terminal pager redirection. Interactive terminal output otherwise invokes the environment pager specified in \fB$PAGER\fR or falls back to \fBless -RFX\fR.
.TP
\fB--min\fR, \fB--min-entropy\fR=\fIFLOAT\fR
Only output lines/blocks with a rolling entropy value greater than or equal to \fIFLOAT\fR (range: 0.0 to 8.0).
.TP
\fB--max\fR, \fB--max-entropy\fR=\fIFLOAT\fR
Only output lines/blocks with a rolling entropy value less than or equal to \fIFLOAT\fR (range: 0.0 to 8.0).
.TP
\fB-p\fR, \fB--plain\fR
Output in plain hexadecimal format (continuous hex bytes without offset addresses or ASCII representations).
.TP
\fB-k\fR, \fB--seek\fR=\fIOFFSET\fR
Start reading at byte offset \fIOFFSET\fR. Accepts decimal values (e.g., \fB4096\fR), hexadecimal values prefixed with \fB0x\fR (e.g., \fB0x1000\fR), or negative offsets (e.g., \fB-k=-512\fR) to seek relative to the end of a regular file.
.TP
\fB-n\fR, \fB--length\fR=\fIBYTES\fR
Process at most \fIBYTES\fR from the input stream.
.TP
\fB-r\fR, \fB--reverse\fR
Reverse a hex dump stream back into raw binary bytes.
.TP
\fB-R\fR, \fB--restore\fR
Restore a malformed or heavily modified hex dump back into a clean formatted dump representation.
.TP
\fB-m\fR, \fB--magic\fR=\fIFILE\fR
Load custom magic byte signatures from a CSV file (Format: \fIHEX_BYTES,Label\fR). Added default headers in v1.1.0 include 7-Zip, FLAC, BZIP2, and Mach-O (32-bit and 64-bit, LE and BE) signatures.
.TP
\fB--nop\fR, \fB--no-prufer\fR
Bypass PRUFER argument validation, flag typo suggestions, file swap checks, and diagnostic guardrails.
.TP
\fB-d\fR, \fB--debug\fR
Enable debug diagnostics and print detailed section parsing/header dispatch exception logs to \fIstderr\fR.
.TP
\fB-h\fR, \fB--help\fR
Show built-in usage instructions and exit.
.TP
\fB-v\fR, \fB--version\fR
Print program version and license information.
.SH SAFETY GUARDS (PRUFER)
\fBneoxd\fR includes the PRUFER validation subsystem enabled by default to prevent catastrophic data destruction:
.IP (bu 3
\fBTypo Detection:\fR Catches invalid option flags and suggests corrections using Levenshtein distance matching.
.IP (bu 3
\fBFile Swap Prevention:\fR Aborts execution if an existing target file is passed as an output argument while the input file does not exist.
.IP (bu 3
\fBBinary Target Protection:\fR Blocks writing raw hex text directly into known binary extensions (.bin, .elf, .exe, .png, etc.) unless restore mode is active.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fBPAGER\fR
Determines the terminal pager command used for interactive output. Defaults to \fBless -RFX\fR if unset.
.SH EXAMPLES
Inspect a firmware file using 32 octets per line:
.RS
.B neoxd -c 32 firmware.bin
.RE
.PP
Group hex bytes into 2-byte word units:
.RS
.B neoxd -g 2 firmware.bin
.RE
.PP
Search and highlight occurrences of a specific hex signature:
.RS
.B neoxd -S 41424344 target.bin
.RE
.PP
Perform a side-by-side binary comparison of two files:
.RS
.B neoxd --diff version1.bin version2.bin
.RE
.PP
Show only blocks with sudden structural entropy shifts (anomalies):
.RS
.B neoxd --anomalies --anomaly-delta=2.0 target.bin
.RE
.PP
Seek to the last 512 bytes of a file:
.RS
.B neoxd -k=-512 target.bin
.RE
.PP
Generate a structural distribution summary report:
.RS
.B neoxd -s binary.elf
.RE
.PP
Safely write a hex dump to a file without risk of source file destruction:
.RS
.B neoxd -o dump.txt image.jpg
.RE
.PP
Reconstruct binary data from stdin in plain hex format:
.RS
.B cat dump.hex | neoxd -r -p > original.bin
.RE
